# Line endings, where they change behaviour rather than taste.
#
# This repository has core.autocrlf=true on at least one developer machine, so a
# checkout on Windows rewrites LF to CRLF in the working tree. For source that
# is only ever read by Node that is invisible. For two kinds of file it is not.
#
# public/setup.sh is *served over HTTP* and piped straight into a shell — the
# app tells people to run `bash -c "$(curl -fsSL .../setup.sh)"` (server/app.js).
# The server sends whatever is on disk, so a Windows checkout would hand a Mac
# or Linux user a script whose every line ends in a carriage return, and bash
# would carry those into command names and arguments. The failure would look
# like nonsense from a script that is fine in the repository.
#
# The shell scripts under worker/ and scripts/ are executed on POSIX machines
# for the same reason.
*.sh text eol=lf

# PowerShell is fine either way, but it is executed rather than read, so it is
# pinned too rather than left to a global setting nobody remembers.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Everything else follows whatever the checkout is configured for; git still
# stores LF. Declared explicitly so `text` is not guessed per file.
* text=auto
